---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const pieces = (await getCollection('research')).sort(
  (a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf(),
);
const latest = pieces.slice(0, 3);
const fmt = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-GB', { dateStyle: 'long' });
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Brandure"
  description="Brandure is an AEO agency. We measure where brands appear in AI-generated answers — ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Perplexity — and do the work that changes it."
>
  <h1>Your buyers are asking an AI. You are not in the answer.</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Brandure is an Answer Engine Optimisation agency. We measure where a brand
    stands inside AI-generated answers, against named competitors, and then do
    the work that moves it.
  </p>

  <h2>Search stopped being a list</h2>

  <p>
    A search engine returns ten links and lets the buyer choose. An answer
    engine returns a recommendation and has already chosen. By the time your
    category comes up in ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini or Perplexity, the shortlist
    is made — and either you are on it or you are not.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is a different problem from ranking, and it has different levers:
    whether a model can resolve who you are, which third-party sources it draws
    on when it answers, and whether your content can be lifted cleanly into a
    reply.
  </p>

  <h2>Measurement first</h2>

  <p>
    We start by showing you the position you actually hold. A defined set of
    buying-intent prompts, run across the major answer engines, recorded as
    which brands get named, in what order, and which sources the answer was
    built from.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is a diagnosis, not a pitch deck. Sometimes it shows a category already
    locked by two incumbents, in which case we will say so.
    <a href="/service">See what an engagement covers</a>.
  </p>

  {
    latest.length > 0 && (
      <>
        <h2>Research</h2>
        <p>
          We publish what we measure. Original scans of how answer engines treat
          specific categories and markets.
        </p>
        <ul class="card-list">
          {latest.map((piece) => (
            <li>
              <p class="meta">
                <time datetime={piece.data.date.toISOString().slice(0, 10)}>
                  {fmt.format(piece.data.date)}
                </time>
                <span class="tag">{piece.data.vertical}</span>
              </p>
              <h3><a href={`/research/${piece.id}`}>{piece.data.title}</a></h3>
              <p>{piece.data.summary}</p>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p><a href="/research">All research</a></p>
      </>
    )
  }
</BaseLayout>
